'''2.''' An encounter between two persons; a duel or single combat.—'''3.''' A fight or encounter between animals, especially when pitted against each other for the amusement of spectators.
{{smaller block|<poem>If we draw lots, he [Cæsar] speeds:
His cocks do win the ''battle'' still of mine,
When it is all to nought; and his quails ever
Beat mine, inhoop’d, at odds.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., ii. 3.}}}}
'''4.''' Warfare; hostile action; actual conflict with enemies: as, wounds received or honors gained in ''battle.''
{{smaller block|Who is this King of glory? The Lord strong and mighty, the Lord mighty in ''battle.'' {{right|Ps. xxiv. 8.}}}}
'''5.''' Any contest or conflict; struggle for mastery or victory: as, the ''battle'' of life.
{{smaller block|Of the six genera, Drosera has been incomparably the most successful in the ''battle'' for life; and a large part of its success may be attributed to its manner of catching insects. {{right|''Darwin'', Insectiv. Plants, p. 357.}}}}
'''6†.''' An army prepared for or engaged in fight.
{{smaller block|<poem>Fire answers fire, and through their paly flames
Each ''battle'' sees the other’s umber’d face.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., iv. (cho.).}}}}

